def bounding_box(points):
    x = [p[0] for p in points]
    y = [p[1] for p in points]
    if len(x) == 1:
        mx, my = x[0], y[0]
        return (mx, my, mx, my)
    mx0, mx1 = min(x), max(x)
    my0, my1 = min(y), max(y)
    return (mx0, my0, mx1, my1)
```

The function collects all the *x* and *y* coordinates of the points and returns two extremes per axis. When there is only one point, both coordinates of the result equal that point’s value, which is the tightest box. Otherwise it takes the smallest and largest *x* as the first and third values and the smallest and largest *y* as the second and fourth, in order of the tuple shown in the first example. All three asserts produce correct outputs under both branches.

