The test is a well-known trick from geometry: when three points lie on a straight line, the slope from the first to the second equals the slope from the second to the third. The function maps each point to its (x, y) coordinates and computes that slope as a pair of differences, returning (1, 0) whenever the x difference is zero so that nearly vertical lines such as the y axis still get a consistent comparison. Two pairs of differences are equal only when both x and y differences agree, so the check is correct over all real coordinates, and the second and third arguments matter only through the line that joins them, not through the other points.

