The sign is set to the first nonzero orientation and then never changes. A zero cross product signals three collinear vertices and returns False directly, as does any later orientation that differs from the first. Otherwise the function returns True.

No particular orientation is assumed, and vertices in random order would otherwise fail even if the shape were convex.

The three-point case walks the polygon twice and returns True, returning False only if the three points lie on a straight line, which is less than the stated requirement for a general polygon but still consistent, since three points on a line cannot enclose any interior area.

