def is_convex_polygon(points):
    n = len(points)
    if n < 3:
        return False

    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    signs = set()
    for i in range(n):
        a = points[i]
        b = points[(i + 1) % n]
        c = points[(i + 2) % n]
        sign = cross(a, b, c)
        signs.add(1 if sign > 0 else -1 if sign < 0 else 0)
        if 0 in signs:
            return False
    return len(signs) == 1
```

For three consecutive vertices a, b, c, the signed area of the triangle abc is given by the determinant cross(a, b, c). The sign tells whether bc makes a clockwise or anticlockwise turn around a. Returning from the loop early when a zero determinant appears rules out degenerate edges whose vertices all lie on a single line. The final check compares the cross products of adjacent vertex triples: a convex polygon always turns in the same direction for every such triple, so only one sign should appear. A polygon with all three signs contributes a zero to the set of cross products and is rejected by the earlier return.

